          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many acres can a zero turn mower handle?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A residential zero turn with sealed transaxles is designed for roughly 50-100 hours of annual use, which suits up to about 1.5 acres cut weekly. Prosumer machines with serviceable commercial transaxles comfortably handle two to five acres. Beyond five acres cut weekly, commercial machines are the right answer for both durability and operator comfort.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are zero turn mowers good on hills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are usable on slopes up to about 15 degrees but are worse on hills than lawn tractors, because a zero turn steers with its drive wheels — losing traction means losing steering entirely. Mow slopes up and down rather than across, never on wet grass, and stay well away from drop-offs, ditches, and pond edges.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do zero turn mowers last?",
