          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure Your Gates First</h3>
          <p>
            The most common sizing regret we hear is a 60-inch deck that does not fit through a 56-inch fence gate. Measure every gate, gap between structures, and trailer ramp you will need to pass through, and remember that overall machine width is typically 2-6 inches wider than the deck itself because of the discharge chute and anti-scalp wheels.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Deck Width to Terrain, Not Just Acreage</h3>
          <p>
            Wide decks bridge across dips and scalp high spots. If your ground is genuinely undulating rather than flat, a 54-inch deck will produce a visibly better cut than a 60-inch one on the same property, at the cost of a few extra minutes per acre. Flat and open rewards width; rough and rolling rewards restraint.
