        <!-- SECTION 5: The Specs That Actually Matter -->
        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Zero Turn Mower Specs That Actually Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spec sheets emphasize horsepower and top speed. Neither is the thing that determines whether you are happy with the machine in year five.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transaxles: The Single Biggest Durability Factor</h3>
          <p>
            The hydrostatic transaxles drive each rear wheel and are the most common expensive failure point. Sealed EZT units found on residential mowers cannot be serviced — when they wear out, you replace them, typically $700-1,100 per side. Commercial ZT-3100, ZT-3400, and ZT-5400 units have serviceable fluid and filters and routinely reach 1,500+ hours. If you are choosing between two machines at similar prices, the transaxle is where to look first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fabricated vs Stamped Decks</h3>
          <p>
            A stamped deck is pressed from a single sheet, cheaper to make and lighter. A fabricated deck is welded from heavier plate, holds its shape under impact, and typically has deeper deck geometry that improves airflow and therefore cut quality in thick grass. In our overgrown-section test, the fabricated decks left visibly cleaner cuts with less stringing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Greaseable Spindles</h3>
          <p>
            Blade spindles take enormous side load. Sealed spindles are maintenance-free until they fail; greaseable ones let you add grease each season and last dramatically longer. It is a small spec line that predicts a lot about how a machine ages.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seat and Suspension</h3>
          <p>
            Underrated until you have mowed for three hours. Zero turns transmit ground shock directly through the frame, and by hour two the difference between a basic seat and a suspension seat stops being a luxury question. If you are cutting more than two acres at a time, prioritize this over almost any other comfort feature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Engine Brand Over Horsepower Number</h3>
          <p>
            A 23hp Kawasaki FR or Kohler Command engine will outlast a 26hp budget engine and hold its power under load better. Horsepower matters only insofar as it prevents the deck bogging in heavy grass; above about 22hp for a 54-inch deck, engine quality matters far more than the number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Slopes, Safety, and Where Zero Turns Struggle -->
        <div id="slopes-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Zero Turn Mowers on Hills: Slopes, Safety, and Where They Struggle
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Slope Rule Nobody Enforces Until It Is Too Late</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Most manufacturers rate zero turn mowers for a maximum 15-degree slope, and that rating assumes dry grass and correct technique. Because a zero turn steers with its drive wheels, losing traction on a hill means losing steering entirely — the machine slides, and there is nothing to correct with. Mow slopes up and down rather than across, never mow wet slopes, and stay off anything near a drop-off, pond edge, or ditch.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond slope, a few habits matter. Keep the roll-over protection bar up and the seatbelt fastened whenever you are on uneven ground — the ROPS only protects you if you are held in the seat. Slow down before turning at the end of a pass; a fast zero turn pivot on soft turf tears divots that take weeks to recover. And treat reverse as a deliberate action rather than a reflex, because visibility behind a zero turn is genuinely poor and this is where most property damage happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other honest limitation is towing. Zero turns can pull light carts and small dethatchers, but a loaded trailer overwhelms the independent-wheel drive, especially downhill, where the load pushes the machine and the drive wheels lose the authority to steer. If regular hauling is part of your plan, keep a garden tractor or UTV for that job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Maintenance That Keeps a Zero Turn Cutting Well -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Zero Turn Mower Maintenance: What Keeps It Cutting Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Zero turn mowers are not fussy, but they are unforgiving of two specific neglects: dull blades and an unlevel deck. Both produce a visibly bad cut that owners often blame on the machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sharpen Blades Every 20-25 Hours</h3>
          <p>
            A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it, leaving frayed white tips that brown within a day and make an expensive mower look cheap. Keep a second set of blades so you can swap in sharp ones in ten minutes and sharpen the dull set at your leisure. Balance blades after sharpening — an unbalanced blade vibrates the spindle to death.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Level the Deck Once a Season</h3>
          <p>
            Decks drift out of level from impacts and tire pressure changes, producing a stepped or uneven cut. Check tire pressures first — this alone fixes many complaints — then measure blade tip height front and rear on both sides against the manufacturer's spec. Most decks want a slight forward rake of about a quarter inch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clean the Deck Underside</h3>
          <p>
            Packed clippings under the deck destroy airflow, which is what lifts grass for a clean cut and moves clippings out. Scrape it after wet mowing rather than letting it dry into concrete, and the cut quality difference is immediately visible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grease, Change Oil, and Watch the Hydro Fluid</h3>
          <p>
            Hit the grease zerks every 25 hours, change engine oil per the manual (typically 50-100 hours), and if you have serviceable transaxles, change the hydro fluid and filters at the interval specified — usually 250-500 hours. That last item is the one owners skip and the one that most reliably prevents a four-figure transaxle repair.
          </p>
        </div>

