            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size zero turn mower do I need for 2 acres?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 52 to 54-inch deck is the sweet spot for two acres, cutting the property in roughly 30 minutes while remaining narrow enough to fit through most gates. A 60-inch deck cuts perhaps five minutes faster but is genuinely wide and scalps more on uneven ground.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many acres can a zero turn mower handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A residential zero turn with sealed transaxles is designed for roughly 50-100 hours of annual use, which suits up to about 1.5 acres cut weekly. Prosumer machines with serviceable commercial transaxles comfortably handle two to five acres. Beyond five acres cut weekly, commercial machines are the right answer for both durability and operator comfort.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are zero turn mowers good on hills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are usable on slopes up to about 15 degrees but are worse on hills than lawn tractors, because a zero turn steers with its drive wheels — losing traction means losing steering entirely. Mow slopes up and down rather than across, never on wet grass, and stay well away from drop-offs, ditches, and pond edges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do zero turn mowers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Residential machines typically last 500-800 hours before major drivetrain wear. Prosumer machines with serviceable transaxles routinely reach 1,500-2,000 hours with proper fluid changes. Commercial units are built for 3,000+ hours. For a homeowner cutting two acres weekly, that translates to roughly 40-60 hours a year, so a prosumer machine can realistically last decades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a fabricated deck worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For thick grass and rough ground, yes. Fabricated decks are welded from heavier steel with deeper geometry that moves more air, which lifts grass for a cleaner cut and disperses clippings better. In our overgrown-grass testing the difference in finish quality was clearly visible. On a flat, well-maintained suburban lawn cut weekly, a stamped deck performs nearly as well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a zero turn mower or a lawn tractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a zero turn if your property has trees, beds, and obstacles to mow around, since eliminating trimming passes is where the time savings come from. Buy a lawn tractor if your land is mostly sloped, or if you need one machine that also tows carts, dethatchers, and spreaders — zero turns are poor at towing under load.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying a Zero Turn Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Zero turns are a large purchase that people typically make once a decade, which is exactly why the same mistakes repeat.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Horsepower Instead of Drivetrain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 26hp engine on sealed residential transaxles will outlive its own drivetrain. The transaxles are the expensive failure point, and whether they are serviceable commercial units or sealed residential ones tells you far more about how the machine will age than the engine number on the sticker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Deck Too Wide for the Property</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 60-inch deck cuts faster in the open and scalps every high spot on undulating ground, plus it will not fit through a 56-inch gate. Measure gates and be honest about how flat your land actually is before assuming wider is better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Seat Comfort Until Hour Two</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zero turns transmit ground shock straight through the frame into the operator. On a showroom floor every seat feels fine. After two hours on rough ground, the difference between a basic seat and a suspension seat is the difference between finishing the job and quitting halfway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing Slopes Sideways</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cutting across a hill feels natural and is the most dangerous thing you can do on a zero turn, because a traction loss on the downhill drive wheel takes your steering with it. Mow up and down slopes, keep the ROPS up and the belt on, and stay off wet hills entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Blade Sharpening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull blade tears grass instead of slicing it, leaving frayed tips that brown within a day. Owners then blame the mower for a bad cut that a ten-minute blade swap would fix. Keep a second sharpened set and rotate them every 20-25 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

